stream
BT
/F1 24 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 516 Tm
(Continental Drift) Tj
/F1 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 476 Tm
(Х In 1912 Alfred Wegener proposed that the continents had once been joined.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 446 Tm
(Х He called the former supercontinent Pangaea.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 416 Tm
(Х Matching coastlines, fossils and rock types supported his idea.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 386 Tm
(Х Most geologists rejected it because he could not explain the driving force.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 356 Tm
(Х Later discoveries on the ocean floor showed that he was largely right.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 326 Tm
(Х Glacial deposits and coal beds were found in places with very different climates) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 306 Tm
(  today.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
10 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 792 612] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 11 0 R >>
endobj
11 0 obj
<< /Length 639 >>
stream
BT
/F1 24 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 516 Tm
(Sea-Floor Spreading) Tj
/F1 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 476 Tm
(Х Mid-ocean ridges are long underwater mountain chains.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 446 Tm
(Х At the ridges, magma rises and forms new oceanic crust.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 416 Tm
(Х The new crust moves away from the ridge on both sides.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 386 Tm
(Х Rocks on the sea floor record reversals of the magnetic field in stripes.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 356 Tm
(Х The stripes are symmetric around the ridge, which confirms the spreading.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 326 Tm
(Х The youngest oceanic crust lies at the ridge and gets older with distance from) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 306 Tm
(  it.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
12 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 792 612] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 13 0 R >>
endobj
13 0 obj
<< /Length 595 >>
stream
BT
/F1 24 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 516 Tm
(Divergent Boundaries) Tj
/F1 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 476 Tm
(Х At divergent boundaries, two plates move away from each other.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 446 Tm
(Х Most divergent boundaries lie along mid-ocean ridges.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 416 Tm
(Х On land they can form rift valleys, such as the East African Rift.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 386 Tm
(Х Iceland sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and has frequent eruptions.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 356 Tm
(Х Earthquakes here are usually shallow and relatively weak.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 326 Tm
(Х Over time a rift valley can widen and flood to become a new ocean.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
14 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 792 612] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 15 0 R >>
endobj
15 0 obj
<< /Length 648 >>
stream
BT
/F1 24 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 516 Tm
(Convergent Boundaries) Tj
/F1 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 476 Tm
(Х At convergent boundaries, two plates move towards each other.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 446 Tm
(Х When an oceanic plate meets a continental plate, the denser oceanic plate sinks.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 416 Tm
(Х This sinking is called subduction and creates deep ocean trenches.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 386 Tm
(Х Subduction feeds chains of volcanoes, such as those around the Pacific.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 356 Tm
(Х When two continents collide, high mountains like the Himalayas form.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 326 Tm
(Х The deepest point on Earth, the Mariana Trench, formed at a subduction zone.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 792 612] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 17 0 R >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /Length 577 >>
stream
BT
/F1 24 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 516 Tm
(Transform Boundaries) Tj
/F1 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 476 Tm
(Х At transform boundaries, plates slide past each other sideways.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 446 Tm
(Х Crust is neither created nor destroyed at these boundaries.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 416 Tm
(Х Friction makes the plates lock and then slip suddenly.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 386 Tm
(Х Each sudden slip releases energy as an earthquake.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 356 Tm
(Х The San Andreas Fault in California is a well-known example.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 326 Tm
(Х Rocks along the fault are often ground into fine crushed material.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /Type /Page /Parent 2 0 R /MediaBox [0 0 792 612] /Resources << /Font << /F1 3 0 R >> >> /Contents 19 0 R >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /Length 631 >>
stream
BT
/F1 24 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 516 Tm
(What Drives the Plates) Tj
/F1 14 Tf
1 0 0 1 72 476 Tm
(Х Heat inside the Earth sets up slow convection currents in the mantle.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 446 Tm
(Х Ridge push occurs as new crust slides down from the raised ridge.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 416 Tm
(Х Slab pull occurs as a sinking plate drags the rest of the plate behind it.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 386 Tm
(Х Slab pull is thought to be the strongest of these forces.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 356 Tm
(Х Scientists still debate how the forces combine in different places.) Tj
1 0 0 1 72 326 Tm
(Х Laboratory models and computer simulations are used to test these ideas.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
